# sekscan {{.Version}} — {{.Target}}

A local/CI security CLI with managed external scanners, SBOMs, application-license
policies, an embedded dashboard, and SQLite/PostgreSQL/SQL Server scan history.

This ZIP contains the **sekscan executable, docs, database schemas, and a clean
workspace**. Scanner executables and vulnerability databases are **not bundled**;
use `prepare` on a connected machine before scanning or copying an offline workspace.
The executable is unsigned. Cross-compilation is not a native-platform runtime test.
See `BUILD-INFO.json`, the outer `release.json`, and `TESTING.md` for validation details.

`README.md` and the requested `readme.med` contain these same instructions.

## 1. Verify and extract

Verify the downloaded ZIP against the release's `SHA256SUMS.txt`, then extract the
entire `{{.Directory}}/` directory. Do not move only the executable after preparation.
Hashes detect corruption but do not authenticate a publisher; obtain release metadata
through a trusted channel. On Unix, restore executable permission if the extractor lost it:

```sh
cd {{.Directory}}
chmod +x sekscan
./sekscan version
```

Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
Set-Location {{.Directory}}
.\sekscan.exe version
```

Use the binary matching your OS and architecture. It is named `{{.Executable}}` in this ZIP.
Windows ARM64 is a separate native application target, not the Windows x64 executable.
Scanner availability is independent: each tool and runtime needs a compatible
upstream distribution. Not every scanner publishes a Windows ARM64 asset.
Preparation reports this limitation; it does not substitute an x64 or system binary.

## 2. Prepare the portable tools and their data

```sh
./sekscan config validate --home .
./sekscan prepare --home . --all --update-tools --with-java --yes
./sekscan doctor --home . --all
./sekscan db status --home . --all --with-java
```

Windows uses identical arguments with `.\sekscan.exe` in place of `./sekscan`.
For example:

```powershell
.\sekscan.exe prepare --home . --all --update-tools --with-java --yes
.\sekscan.exe doctor --home . --all
```

Managed copies are installed into `bin/` even if the tools are already on PATH.
Normal scans do not silently update executables. `--all` selects configured tools,
not every possible scanner. The expanded suite includes Hadolint and conditional
source/workflow checks. Approved preparation installs the managed Go SDK and scanner
runtimes as needed, but not arbitrary custom runtimes or PostgreSQL/SQL Server services.
Compatible native distributions are required; see docs/DEFAULT-SCANNERS.md and
TESTING.md. Pin reviewed tool versions with `deps lock` for CI.

Existing explicit false flags remain unchanged. To enable the expanded suite without
replacing an old config, use `config enable-default-scanners --config sekscan.json
--out sekscan-expanded.json`, then select the new file during preparation and scanning.
Go modules with uncached dependencies need explicit `prepare --go-module PATH --yes`;
this may update the project's go.sum.

## 3. Scan: a project name is required

```sh
./sekscan scan dir:/path/to/source --project payments-api \
  --branch main --revision COMMIT_ID --out security-report
./sekscan serve security-report
```

```powershell
.\sekscan.exe scan 'dir:C:\work\payments-api' --project payments-api `
  --branch main --revision COMMIT_ID --out security-report
.\sekscan.exe serve security-report
```

Project names are stable database keys within a namespace. Reuse exactly the same
name on every agent. `project.key` in `sekscan.json` may supply the name instead of
`--project`; an empty key is rejected even for SQLite or `--no-store` scans.
Use explicit branches and target-kind filters when reviewing trends.

Scan a built image or local archive:

```sh
./sekscan scan image:my-service:build-123 --project payments-api --out image-report
./sekscan scan docker-archive:./image.tar --project payments-api --offline --out archive-report
```

An offline scan requires all scanner data and local target/dependency content to
be prepared already. A Dockerfile by itself is not a scanned image.

**Exit codes:** 0 = completed/pass; 1 = completed/policy failure; 2 = error or
incomplete required coverage. Run `serve` separately and publish artifacts even
when the scan fails. Never use `|| true` to disguise the scan status in CI.

## 4. Project history, paging, trends, and comparison

```sh
./sekscan serve
./sekscan history projects --page 1 --page-size 25
./sekscan history list --project payments-api --page 2 --page-size 25 --json
./sekscan history trends --project payments-api --branch main --kind dir --trend-limit 100 --json
./sekscan history compare BASE_RUN_ID HEAD_RUN_ID --project payments-api --page-size 25
./sekscan history compare BASE_RUN_ID HEAD_RUN_ID --view components --json
./sekscan history compare BASE_RUN_ID HEAD_RUN_ID --out comparison.json
```

The history dashboard opens with **Portfolio → Projects → Dashboard / Runs → Run details**. Use run-row
buttons to select an earlier baseline and later candidate across pages. The project
dashboard plots findings, severities, categories, inventory size, and elapsed time.
First/previous/next/last links, a page jump, and page-size controls accompany lists.

Comparison reports new, changed, unchanged, no-longer-reported, and unverified
findings. Incomplete or changed coverage must not appear as remediation. Changed
package versions have separate identities; no-longer-reported does not prove a fix.
See `docs/HISTORY.md` for comparison limits and API pagination envelopes.

## 5. Workspace and configuration

```text
{{.Directory}}/
  {{.Executable}}        Application executable
  sekscan.json           Main settings (project.key is intentionally empty)
  config/                Standard native scanner and integration configs
  bin/                   Managed scanner executables, populated by prepare
  cache/                 Scanner databases, populated by prepare
  logs/                  JSONL logs (never publish private diagnostic logs)
  data/                  SQLite history database, created when used
  docs/                  Architecture, security, history, portability, release guides
  schema/                SQLite, PostgreSQL, SQL Server bootstrap schemas
  examples/              Configuration, synthetic demo, CI examples
  SOURCE-README.md        Complete source README
  BUILD-INFO.json         Target/version/binary digest
  GO-MODULES.json         Resolved application module graph
  SHA256SUMS.txt          Checksums for files inside this package
```

Configuration lookup is explicit `--config`, then `SEKSCAN_CONFIG`, then
`sekscan.json`/`config/sekscan.json` in the working folder and beside the executable.
Use `--home DIR` for an explicit workspace. In CI select a trusted config explicitly;
never allow a pull request to substitute its own scanner definitions or policy.

SQLite is local and portable. To share results across machines, select `postgres`
or `mssql` and supply the DSN through the configured environment variable. Use
`storage migrate` with a migration identity; scan jobs should have only necessary
permissions. See `docs/DATABASE.md`. The existing schema v1 already keys projects
by namespace/name and needs no migration for history/comparison features.

## 6. Portability and updates

```sh
./sekscan deps check --home . --all --json
./sekscan prepare --home . --all --update-tools --with-java --yes
./sekscan storage backup --home . --out history-backup.db
```

To transfer an offline workspace, stop scans/updates/database writers and copy the
whole folder to the same OS/architecture. SQLite needs a consistent copy; external
PostgreSQL/SQL Server databases are not copied with the folder. Scanner data is
separate from scan-history storage. Native security controls are not an OS sandbox.

## 7. Troubleshooting and source builds

For a scanner failure, first use `doctor` and `db status`. Syft failure blocks
Grype's SBOM-dependent scan. `--diagnostic-stderr` writes raw private logs that may
contain secrets; never upload them blindly. See `docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md`.

Source release builds use `scripts/build-release.sh` or `scripts/build-release.ps1`:

```sh
bash scripts/build-release.sh --version {{.Version}} --targets all
```

```powershell
.\scripts\build-release.ps1 --version {{.Version}} --targets all
```

Run these from the separate source checkout/package, not this runtime ZIP. Resolve,
review, and commit `go.mod`/`go.sum` first. The release builder does not update them,
requires production drivers, and refuses test-only binaries. See `docs/RELEASING.md`.


## Batch and GitHub modes

Create `projects.json` using `schema/batch.schema.json`:

```json
{"version":1,"projects":[
  {"project":"payments-api","path":"../payments-api"},
  {"repo_url":"https://github.com/mictsi/SekuraDesignMCP"}
]}
```

```sh
./sekscan batch validate projects.json
./sekscan batch projects.json --home . --out batch-reports
./sekscan github mictsi/SekuraDesignMCP --home .
./sekscan serve --home .
```

Relative source paths are relative to the manifest. GitHub keys default to lowercase
owner/repository and are recorded with the resolved commit. Tokens come from the
environment, never JSON. GitHub snapshots use a portable source cache and do not
require Git. For offline runs, use an exact already-cached commit. No repository
scripts are executed. Read `docs/BATCH.md` for partial-source coverage and limits.

Without a report directory, `serve` starts the portfolio Dashboard. Projects and
All runs are separate tabs, as are project Dashboards / Runs and comparison Summary /
Findings / Components. Reports use the same embedded Sekura design, credited in
`docs/UI.md` and `third_party/SekuraDesignMCP-LICENSE`.


## Sekura 3.0.2 presentation

History and report pages share the same compact shell. Navigation expansion is
separate from route selection. The mobile navigation drawer closes with Escape and
returns focus; its background is blocked while open. Filter rows align labels,
hints, controls and feedback. Use Table density for comfortable, compact or dense
rows without changing the rest of the page. Appearance includes System and four
explicit themes.

Stored history renders with this executable's embedded UI. Previously exported
HTML remains unchanged until regenerated from its results JSON:

```sh
./sekscan report ./old-report/results.json --out ./refreshed-report
```

Use a new output directory and keep the original SBOM files. No database migration
or scanner update is required for this presentation change. See
`docs/DESIGN-MIGRATION-3.0.2.md` and `TESTING.md` for the validation boundary.

## Removed scanner

Semgrep is no longer installed, tested or scanned. Legacy configuration entries are
ignored with a migration warning; stored results and unused workspace files remain.
See `docs/SEMGREP-REMOVAL.md`. No Python package/runtime setup is needed for the
remaining default suite.
